1. DEFINITIONS

1.1 "RIBOSPAN Model Materials"

"RIBOSPAN Model Materials" or "Model Materials" means RIBOSPAN model weights, pretrained checkpoints, model parameters, model-specific configuration files, weight indexes, tokenizer assets distributed as part of a model release, and other model-specific materials expressly distributed by the Licensor under this License.

For clarity, source code separately released under the Apache License 2.0 is not included in the Model Materials.

1.2 "Derivative Model"

"Derivative Model" means any model, checkpoint, or set of model parameters derived from, adapted from, or materially based on the RIBOSPAN Model Materials, including models produced through:

(a) fine-tuning;

(b) continued pretraining;

(c) parameter-efficient fine-tuning;

(d) adapters, LoRA, or similar parameter modifications;

(e) model merging;

(f) pruning;

(g) quantization;

(h) weight interpolation;

(i) model conversion; or

(j) any other modification or adaptation of the Model Materials.

A model that uses RIBOSPAN representations or Outputs as training data, features, supervision, or other inputs, without incorporating, reproducing, or modifying RIBOSPAN parameters, is not by that fact alone a Derivative Model. Such a model may constitute an Output-Derived Model under Section 1.5.

1.3 "Output"

"Output" means information generated, inferred, or computed through use of the RIBOSPAN Model Materials, including, without limitation:

(a) nucleotide probabilities or scores;

(b) logits;

(c) embeddings;

(d) hidden representations;

(e) attention-related representations;

(f) annotations or predictions;

(g) variant scores;

(h) rankings or selections of nucleotide sequences or biological candidates;

(i) generated or optimized nucleotide sequences;

(j) candidate biological sequences or designs; and

(k) other representations, predictions, scores, rankings, selections, or model-generated results.

Data, sequences, molecules, designs, or other materials supplied by a user as input to RIBOSPAN do not become Outputs merely because they are processed, scored, embedded, ranked, predicted upon, or evaluated using RIBOSPAN.

1.4 "Output Derivative"

"Output Derivative" means any sequence, molecule, design, dataset, representation, candidate, selection, prioritization, decision result, or other result materially generated, modified, optimized, developed, selected, prioritized, or created on the basis of one or more Outputs.

An independently owned user input does not become an Output Derivative merely because it is processed, scored, embedded, ranked, predicted upon, or evaluated using RIBOSPAN.

However, a selection, ranking, prioritization, modification, optimization, design decision, or development decision materially based on one or more RIBOSPAN Outputs may itself constitute an Output or Output Derivative and remains subject to the applicable restrictions of this License.

1.5 "Output-Derived Model"

"Output-Derived Model" means any model, predictor, classifier, regressor, scoring system, representation model, or other machine-learning system materially trained, fine-tuned, optimized, calibrated, or otherwise developed using RIBOSPAN Outputs or representations, without incorporating, reproducing, or modifying RIBOSPAN parameters.

Examples include task-specific models materially trained or developed using RIBOSPAN embeddings, hidden representations, logits, scores, annotations, predictions, or other Outputs.

An Output-Derived Model is not a Derivative Model solely because it was developed using RIBOSPAN Outputs.

A general-purpose model intended to reproduce, substitute for, or materially replicate substantial RIBOSPAN capabilities remains subject to Section 5 regardless of whether it may otherwise qualify as an Output-Derived Model.

1.6 "Separable Adaptation"

"Separable Adaptation" means a separately distributable task-specific adapter, LoRA parameter set, parameter-efficient fine-tuning module, task-specific head, or similar model component developed from or for use with RIBOSPAN Model Materials that does not include the underlying full RIBOSPAN checkpoint or a substantial portion of its parameters and cannot reasonably be used by itself to reconstruct, reproduce, or substitute for the underlying checkpoint.

A Separable Adaptation that is derived from, adapts, or modifies RIBOSPAN parameters may constitute a Derivative Model. The designation of a component as a Separable Adaptation does not exempt it from any otherwise applicable requirement of this License and affects distribution only to the extent expressly provided in Sections 10 and 11.

1.7 "Restricted-Access Checkpoint"

"Restricted-Access Checkpoint" means any RIBOSPAN checkpoint that the Licensor designates as subject to access approval, access conditions, identity or affiliation verification, or another controlled distribution process.

A checkpoint may be designated as a Restricted-Access Checkpoint regardless of the technical platform, hosting service, application procedure, or distribution mechanism through which access is provided.

1.8 "Non-Commercial Research Purpose"

"Non-Commercial Research Purpose" means use primarily for:

(a) academic or scientific research;

(b) education or teaching;

(c) model evaluation;

(d) benchmarking;

(e) reproducibility studies;

(f) experimentation; or

(g) scientific publication,

provided that such use is not intended for, directed toward, or materially supporting commercial advantage, revenue generation, commercial product development, commercial service provision, technology commercialization, or other commercial exploitation.

Research performed by an academic, educational, governmental, or non-profit organization is not automatically a Non-Commercial Research Purpose where the research is performed primarily for the commercial benefit of a for-profit entity or is materially directed toward the development, validation, optimization, regulatory advancement, or commercialization of a commercial product, service, platform, or technology.

1.9 "Commercial Use"

"Commercial Use" means any use that is intended for, directed toward, or materially supports commercial advantage, revenue generation, commercial product development, commercial service provision, technology commercialization, commercial research or development, or other commercial exploitation.

Commercial Use includes the activities described in Section 3.

